// Reassigning an `owned` `var` that was never moved freed nothing: the old
// value leaked on every `v = ...`. Not an R11 "Still broken" row (R11 is
// about `arc`); OWNERSHIP.md's R16 paragraph carried it together with the
// revival leak, and `emitAssign`'s pre-drop was `arc`-only because a list
// literal `[s]` once copied the header without marking `s` moved, so freeing
// the old value would have freed under that element. borrowck refuses that
// list element since c314a0e, and no other untracked alias of an `owned`
// String or list is accepted.
//
// Each iteration reassigns a String var and a list var once each, so the
// pre-fix count is 2000 on both witnesses.
//
// NOT covered, by design: a var that is moved ANYWHERE in the function,
// before the reassignment (R3a revival, where the old value may belong to
// someone else) or after it (a later `return v` or `take(v)`, because
// borrowck's `wasMoved` is not flow-sensitive). No pre-drop is emitted for
// either and the reassigned-away values still leak.
//
// Status: parses, passes `cell check`. LLVM and MLIR refuse it; lives under
// examples/leaks/ so it is invisible to the top-level corpus, agreement and
// mlir-lowering loops (see examples/leaks/README.md).

pub fn print_int(copy value: Int);

pub fn main() {
    var i = 0
    while i < 1000 {
        var owned s: String = "first-string"
        s = "second-string"
        var owned xs: [Int] = [1, 2, 3]
        xs = [4, 5]
        i = i + 1
    }
    print_int(i)
}
